---
// Download and install, from the README's "Install", "Permissions", "Coming from 0.2", "Not in 1.0"
// and "Support the project", and windows/HOMEPAGE-INSTALL.md for Windows (its UI labels were
// written from Microsoft's documentation and still need checking on a PC: windows/S3.6-CHECKLIST.md).
// The installer's SHA-256 is GitHub's record of the upload, from the release snapshot; the steps
// tell people to compare it with the sums file on the release too.
import Downloads from './Downloads.astro';
import Shot from './Shot.astro';
import { DONATION_SUGGESTED, DONATION_URL, RELEASES_LATEST_URL } from '../lib/constants';
import { DOWNLOADS } from '../lib/release';

const setup = DOWNLOADS.windows.name;
const sums = DOWNLOADS.windowsSums;
const hashCommand = `Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 "$HOME\\Downloads\\${setup}"`;
---

<section class="section band-dark glow-orb" id="download" aria-labelledby="download-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="download-title">Download Inkwell.</h2>
      <p>
        Free: no paid tier, no licence keys, no account. If it saves you time, you can
        <a href={DONATION_URL}>buy me a coffee</a> ({DONATION_SUGGESTED} suggested, entirely voluntary; nothing in the app is
        gated behind it).
      </p>
    </div>
    <Downloads left />

    <div class="install-grid">
      <div>
        <details class="more" data-platform="mac">
          <summary>Installing on the Mac <span class="hint">macOS 26 or later, Apple silicon</span></summary>
          <div class="more-body">
            <ol>
              <li>
                <b>Download</b> <code>{DOWNLOADS.mac.name}</code>, open it and drag Inkwell to Applications. The app is
                signed with a Developer ID, and the app and the disk image are notarized by Apple, so it opens without a
                warning.
              </li>
              <li>
                <b>Open Inkwell.</b> It lives in the menu bar. The first run walks through the permissions, the speech
                models, the import from 0.2 and the optional language model.
              </li>
            </ol>
            <p>While Secure Input is on (in a password field, for example), nothing is typed. The Drop says so, and your words are in the Library.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="more" data-platform="windows">
          <summary>Installing on Windows <span class="hint">Windows 11 24H2 or later, x64</span></summary>
          <div class="more-body">
            <p>Inkwell installs for your user account alone: no administrator password, nothing system-wide.</p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <b>Download</b> <code>{setup}</code> from the <a href={RELEASES_LATEST_URL}>latest release</a>. Your
                browser may say the file “isn’t commonly downloaded”. In Edge, open the download’s <b>…</b> menu, choose
                <b>Keep</b>, then <b>Show more</b> and <b>Keep anyway</b>. In Chrome, choose <b>Keep</b>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <b>Check the download</b> (recommended). The Windows build is not code-signed yet, so this is how you
                know the file is the one that was published. In PowerShell:
                <pre><code>{hashCommand}</code></pre>
                {DOWNLOADS.windows.sha256 ? (
                  <div class="hash">
                    <span class="row">It must print this SHA-256<button type="button" class="copy js-only" data-copy={DOWNLOADS.windows.sha256}>Copy</button></span>
                    <code>{DOWNLOADS.windows.sha256}</code>
                  </div>
                ) : null}
                The hash must match the one in the release notes{sums ? <>, and in <a href={sums.url}><code>{sums.name}</code></a></> : null} on
                the same release. If it does not, delete the file and do not run it.
              </li>
              <li>
                <b>Run it.</b> SmartScreen says “Windows protected your PC”, with an unknown publisher. Choose
                <b>More info</b>, then <b>Run anyway</b>. Inkwell installs in a few seconds, opens, and is in the Start menu.
              </li>
            </ol>
            <p class="warn">
              <b>Smart App Control</b> has no “Run anyway”: it does not run apps that are not signed, and Microsoft offers
              no per-app exception. If it blocks the installer, wait for the signed build rather than turning off a
              protection you chose to keep.
            </p>
            {/* W1 (SmartScreen) goes back in once captured on a PC: scripts/encode-shots.mjs, then placeholder: false. */}
            <p>
              <b>Microsoft’s terms.</b> Inkwell includes the runtime of Microsoft’s Windows App SDK, the Windows SDK’s
              .NET projection and the Visual C++ runtime, which Microsoft licenses separately under the Microsoft
              Software License Terms (shown in full in Inkwell’s Settings > About). By installing or using Inkwell on
              Windows, you agree to those terms for those components. The first run shows them, with Agree and Quit,
              before anything else starts.
            </p>
            <p>
              <b>Updates.</b> Inkwell checks for a new version when you ask: Settings > About > <b>Check Now</b>. Updates
              come from this project’s GitHub releases over HTTPS, and each one’s size and SHA-256 are checked against
              the release before it installs.
            </p>
            <p>
              <b>Uninstalling.</b> Settings > Apps > Installed apps > Inkwell > <b>Uninstall</b>. Your library (meetings,
              dictations, notes and downloaded models) stays in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Inkwell</code>; delete that folder too
              to remove it.
            </p>
            <p>An app running as administrator can’t be typed into, and its keys can’t be heard.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="more">
          <summary>What Inkwell asks for</summary>
          <div class="more-body">
            <dl class="perm">
              <div><dt>Hear you</dt><dd>Microphone, on both. Without it, nothing you say can be written down.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Hear the others</dt><dd>System audio on the Mac; on Windows, no permission to grant. Without it, meetings record only your voice.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Type for you</dt><dd>Accessibility on the Mac, also needed for the dictation key; on Windows, no permission to grant. Without it, dictation can’t type into other apps or hear the key.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Know your meetings</dt><dd>Calendar on the Mac, optional; not available on Windows in 1.0. Without it, meetings are named by the app they were in.</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <p>A permission check never brings up a prompt on its own: Inkwell asks when you press the button for it, and checks again when you come back from the system’s settings.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="more">
          <summary>Coming from 0.2</summary>
          <div class="more-body">
            <p>
              The 0.2 app’s updater cannot install 1.0, so download 1.0 from the release. The first run, and Settings,
              offer to import 0.2’s dictation history, dictionary, snippets, voice commands, modes and settings, with
              your stored API keys linked rather than copied. The Homebrew cask still installs 0.2 for now.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="more">
          <summary>Disk space for the models</summary>
          <div class="more-body">
            <p>
              All the speech models together take about 3.1 GB on the Mac and 3.3 GB on Windows, each downloaded only
              when you choose it: Qwen3-ASR is 2.5 GB of that. The optional language model on Windows is 2.5 GB more.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="side">
        <p>
          <b>Not in 1.0:</b> Linux and Intel Macs (0.2 is their last version), Windows on ARM64, a code-signed Windows
          build, the calendar on Windows, and a Homebrew cask for 1.x.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
